On Mon, 21 Apr 1997 09:47:09 -0500, "Floyd Davenport"
<davenportf@ces.aces.uiuc.edu> wrote:

>I'm running DEC Unix 4.0 and I have some friendly folks that have decided
>to 
>make use of my system without permission.  They are using telnet and ftp to
>
>gain access to the system and drop off some files (IRC bot).  They haven't
>been
>destructive (yet) and the accounts they have used had pretty simple
>passwords
>.
>
>We are in the process of updating our password files, but I wanted to ask
>if
>there might be some other precautions that we might want to take.  For
>example, 
>is there a simple way to reject connections from specific host computers?
>
>Floyd Davenport
>davenportf@idea.ag.uiuc.edu
>
>
There are 2 files in /etc directory called hosts.allow , hosts.deny U
have to setup those files if U want to reject connections from
specific hosts , domains . The syntax is simple :

hosts.allow
==========

ALL: LOCAL, my.domain.com
ALL: LOCAL, 124.10.69.
# End of hosts.allow.


hosts.deny
==========

ALL: .netcom.com, .net.il, .actcom.co.il
ALL: 192.114.47., 147.235.1., 192.116.102., 192.116.206.
# End of hosts.deny.


I hope it'll help U .
Bye.

George Berg 	<george@gilad.co.il>
		<http://gberg.gilad.co.il>



