In article <336516c8.87525299@news.netvision.net.il>,
George Berg <george@gilad.co.il> wrote:
>On Mon, 21 Apr 1997 09:47:09 -0500, "Floyd Davenport"
><davenportf@ces.aces.uiuc.edu> wrote:
>
>>I'm running DEC Unix 4.0 and I have some friendly folks that have decided
>>to 
>>make use of my system without permission.  They are using telnet and ftp to

	[ ... ]

>There are 2 files in /etc directory called hosts.allow , hosts.deny U
>have to setup those files if U want to reject connections from
>specific hosts , domains . The syntax is simple :

	Does tcp-wrappers come standard with DEC's version of unix?  Those
files are part of the installation of tcp-wrappers.  You also need to edit
/etc/inetd.conf properly so the connection requests are passed to tcpd
instead of the usual programs.  "tcpd" then decides whether to allow the
connection, in which case it passes the connection on to the program
specified in /etc/inetd.conf.

	As an example, on my system (a Sun running Solaris 2.x), I have:

telnet stream tcp nowait root /usr/sbin/tcpd		/usr/sbin/in.telnetd

	While a unwrapped system (or one which hadn't bothered wrapping
telnet), would have something like:

telnet stream tcp nowait root /usr/sbin/in.telnetd 	in.telnetd

	Only once this is done can the entries in /etc/hosts.deny and
/etc/hosts.allow do anything.

	You'll probably also want to treat various services differently, so
instead of using "ALL", you would have an entry for in.telnetd, one for
in.ftpd, and for each other service which had specific requirements.  Then,
you might use an ALL entry to catch anything else which slipped through the
cracks.

	Be sure to run tcpdchk, to get it to pass an opinion on how well
configured the files are.

	Good Luck,
		DoN.
