From: Richard H Frobose <frobose@llnl.gov>
Subject: DEC's support for DCE???
Date: 1997/04/29
Message-ID: <33669713.5DD9@llnl.gov>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 238334514
Organization: Lawrence Livermore National Lab
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.soft-sys.dce


We at Livermore (in conjunction with two other government laboratories)
are migrating from our central Kerberos authentication system to a
vendor supported DCE environment.  This entails integrating multiple
vendors' Unix platforms into a common security environment.  We
understood at the start that some vendors would be more difficult to
integrate than others, but based on what we heard from DEC, we expected
our Digital UNIX machines to integrate relatively smoothly into a DCE
environment.  This has not proven to be the case.
 
While we have several issues concerning DEC's Digital UNIX 4.0 support
for DCE, I would like to raise one central issue here, in the hope that
some discussion might shed new light on the issue.  It deals with how
DEC has implemented integrated login (i.e. integrating DCE password
authentication into the user login operation).
 
Digital Unix supports DCE as one of the authentication methods subsumed
under the Security Integration Architecture (SIA).  But unfortunately
we have found that SIA does not allow you to both use DCE
authentication and have C2 security.  This is a serious problem since
C2 security effectively gives you shadow password files.  Without it,
any passwords that are local to a machine are visible to all (in
encrypted form).  Using DCE integrated login, most users need only
passwords in the DCE registry and will not have to expose them in the
/etc/passwd file.  However, system administrators need to be able to
log in to the machine even if DCE is unavailable. These administrators
need to be given local passwords.  But we are not willing to expose the
passwords for root accounts to everyone on the machine.
 
This is a real show stopper for us in our migration efforts.  But in
spite of repeated attempts, so far we have been unable to get DEC to
resolve this issue.  Has anyone had more success?
 
Rich Frobose



