In article <01bc5410$eabb88e0$360101c0@NT1.globalmt.com>, "Jeremy Anderson" <janderson@globalmt.com> writes:
>
>Can anyone help me with how I increase the size/quantity of the filesystem
>buffer for our machines?  Unfortunately, I _am_ the system administrator,
>so going to the SA for help hasn't gotten me much.

Left to itself and given the opportunity, Digital UNIX will use all
of available memory for file system buffer cache.  Unless you've
done something to limit the amount of memory that will be used
for the cache, there isn't anything you can do raise it, except
get more memory.  Or, have applications use less memory.

The buffer cache and applications compete for free memory.  If
you're tight on memory then running something large, will
flush or invalidate the cache to free up memory for the particular
application.  The application is reading or writing large files,
then it may have momentarily idle pages taken away to be used
for the cache.

You can check the upper limit on how much memory will be used
by looking at the kernel variable "ubc_maxpercent":

% dbx -k /vmunix /dev/mem
(dbx) print ubc_maxpercent

You might also be able to check the value with sysconfig.  If
the value is 100, then you're allowing all of memory to be
used for cache.  I think you can change the value using
sysconfig and rebooting, or changing it in the kernel configuration
file, rebuilding and rebooting.

>Thanks for any help you can give me!
>
>Jeremy Anderson

--
Alan Rollow				alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com




