Floyd Davenport (davenportf@ces.aces.uiuc.edu) wrote:
: I'm running DEC Unix 4.0 and I have some friendly folks that have decided
: to make use of my system without permission.  They are using telnet and
: ftp to gain access to the system and drop off some files (IRC bot).
: They haven't been destructive (yet) and the accounts they have used had
: pretty simple passwords.

Eggdrop?  (The IRC Bot)  We encountered something like this a little while
ago, and have undergone several severe security upgrades.  We moved to a
restricted-host setup, where we limit ALL connections to authorized hosts. 
(Available though Linux quite nicely) 

: We are in the process of updating our password files, but I wanted to ask
: if there might be some other precautions that we might want to take.

You should definately move towards a passwd/shadow suite of tools, which
store the passwords in a more secure shadow file.  This would stop the IRC
bot from snarfing your passwd file (along with encrypted passwords) right
off.  The passwords are stored in a root-only file, which is accessed by a
number of tools.

You also may wish to take a look at some passwd-guessing tools, Crack
comes to mind.  SATAN may help you analyse your network problems, but its
use is as a tool - it won't solve all your problems.  COPS is also a good
password/security tool.

Local security should also be a concern, do you exist in a physical
environment which may lend itself to ipsniffing or ipspoofing?

What kind of architecture are you running?

