In article <5jfui3$3ec@vixen.cso.uiuc.edu>,
Floyd Davenport <davenportf@ces.aces.uiuc.edu> wrote:
>I'm running DEC Unix 4.0 and I have some friendly folks that have decided
>to make use of my system without permission.  They are using telnet and ftp
>to gain access to the system and drop off some files (IRC bot).  They haven't
>been destructive (yet) and the accounts they have used had pretty simple
>passwords.
>
>We are in the process of updating our password files, but I wanted to ask if
>there might be some other precautions that we might want to take.  

First off, it's probably a good idea to enable the Enhanced Security
option that comes with Digital UNIX 4.0, as this comes free AFAIK.
This gives you shadowed passwords, more fine-grained control over
user/login/password attributes, 16 significant characters in passwords,
auditing and a whole host of other security features.  There's also a C2
option available, but this might be chargeable.

**_NOTE_**:  Read the documentation carefully before turning this option
on; it's something that needs quite careful configuration to leave you
with a "useable" system, and it's a good idea to know ahead of time what
you want this to provide you with, and how to go about achieving this.  Be
prepared for this option to immediately expire all the administration
passwords, and not allow you to change them back to what they were!

Over and above this, you might like to install a security auditing system
on your machines to check and maintain your security setup.  Examples
are COPS, Digital's own POLYCENTER Security Compliance Manager, or if
you want something more flexible or powerful, our own Security Manager
software.  See the URL in my sig for more details.

>For example, is there a simple way to reject connections from specific
>host computers?

You might like to install something like TCP wrappers locally, or more
generally this sort of function can be better performed by a firewall
system.  Remember, firewalls aren't just for connecting to the internet;
they can be used to compartmentalise internal networks too.

	Shaun.

