Justin B. Harvey <jbharvey@gte.net> writes:

> In comp.security.unix Thomas H. Ptacek <tqbf@char-star.rdist.org> wrote:
> 
> > 22 Apr 1997 16:05:59 GMT jbharvey@FIX.TIN.DOMAIN:
> > >* Possibly run Secure Shell or S/Key
> 
> > Be aware that neither of these two packages are a panacea. S/Key has
> > published conceptual vulnerabilities (the primary one being that
> > authentication alone doesn't resolve the problem of session hijacking),
> > and SSH is a large, complex piece of software that people are just
> > starting to dig into. 
> 
> I'm not up on S/key, but SSH is neither large nor complex.  For his
> purpose I think it'd do well.  If you're talking about security
> vulnerabilities in it, it would at the least make it so that he wouldn't
> be sending his or the root password in clear text across the wire.  I'm
> interested in hearing more detailed thoughts on the two Thomas.

While S/Key has limitations, it is much better than clear
passwords. But ssh is vastly superior to either S/Key or clear
passwords.

Since ssh encrypts the entire session, security is quite good. It
supports most popular encryption algorithms including IDEA,
triple-DES, and Diffie-Hellman. 

Authentication can use RSA public key technology although I understand
that someone has it working with Kerberos. It can also pass encrypted
passwords.

It's fairly easy to install and get running and available for most
Unix platforms as well as Windoze although the Windoze support is from
a commercial provider while the Unix version is free.

In addition to encrypting the terminal session, it also will set up
Xauthorization and a proxy so that X windows data will also be
encrypted. While it's not entirely "mature", it is very functional and
easy to set up and use.
-- 
