>I'm not up on S/key, but SSH is neither large nor complex.  For his

SSH, by itself (sans libraries) is over twenty-six thousand lines of code.

It performs more services than any other Unix login client. Very few
people have actually read the code (there are some issues that would have
been dealt with by now if everyone was reading it). The SSH daemon runs
with privilege, and it's VM space contains sensitive information - yet SSH
happily forks off a copy of itself during user-login and reduces
privilege.

You don't see a potential for problems here?

Contrast SSH with CERT-IT Secure Telnet (and have a look at the code,
while you're at it). Compare it with the (cryptographically inferior)
deslogin system. Have a look at SSLeay/SSL-Telnet. 

Compared to the alternatives, SSH is massive.

