23 Apr 1997 10:13:15 -0700 oberman@es.net:
>While S/Key has limitations, it is much better than clear
>passwords. But ssh is vastly superior to either S/Key or clear
>passwords.

SSH sounds superior to authentication-only systems like S/Key. The
concept of full session encryption is definitely more secure than the
concept of cryptographic authentication and plaintext sessions.

Whether the SSH implementation is more secure than the (relatively simple)
S/Key system remains to be seen. 

[ SSH blurb elided ]

>encrypted. While it's not entirely "mature", it is very functional and
>easy to set up and use.

This is a problem. The SSH package compiles into a pair of binaries that
do a ton of stuff. The more jobs a program has, the more likely it is that
it will screw one of them up. A screw-up in privileged code is a security
hole. 

I would have preferred the SSH system to have split up tasks into multiple
daemons and clients. I'd also like to see a smaller, stripped down client
available somewhere that only handles the "rsh" aspect of SSH, and a
daemon that does likewise.

-- 
