Summary: text database system for data selection and reporting
Organization: Just me.
Keywords: tdb text database
Newsgroups: comp.unix.programmer,comp.sys.hp.apps,comp.sys.hp.hpux,comp.sys.hp.misc,comp.unix.aix,comp.unix.misc,comp.unix.osf.misc,comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.unix.solaris,comp.unix.sys5.misc,comp.unix.ultrix



A while ago, I wrote a program I call tdb, for "text database". It's really
a prototype, at this stage. It allows one to setup non-relational databases
consisting of text data and metadata files, and then access the data  using
a query language.

The language itself is  of  less  interest  to  me  than  the  data  access
methodology.  If  you're  interested,  have  a  look;  I'd like to hear any
constructive feedback.

I am  posting  (gzip-ed  and  uuencoded)  executables  for  AIX_4.1_rs6000,
HPUX_10.10_700,   HPUX_10.10_800,   OSF1_2.0_alpha,   SunOS_5.5_sparc,  and
ULTRIX_4.4_risc, along with a tar file of some examples, documentation  and
a reasonably comprehensive test suite (in comp.unix.programmer).

To run the test suite, first uudecode and unzip the appropriate  executable
and  the  tar file. Rename the executable to "tdb", and put it somewhere in
your execution  path.  Next,  extract  the  "TEST",  "doc"  and  "EXAMPLES"
directories  from  the  tar  file.  Change  directory  into  "TEST" and run
"testall". The output consists of  the  differences  between  my  reference
output files and the ones  generated  on  your  system.  These  differences
should  be  only  date/time  stamps  and  separator lines (23 lines of this
output means everything is working).

To build the documentation, cd into the "doc" directory, and run  nroff  on
tdbquickref.  For  the  security  conscious,  tdb does _not_ need to run as
root.

