Hi!

I am facing a strange problem with Digital Unix (OSF/1) semaphores and
signals  and  wonder  if  any  one  of  you have encountered something
similar.

I  have  two  processes  called  Scheduler  and  Task.  The  Scheduler
schedules  Task  in a deterministic manner. It releases the  semaphore
when it wants to allow the Task to run and sends it a signal  when  it
wants  to  stop  it.  The  Task releases the  semaphore in it's signal
handler, so that the semaphore comes back to the Scheduler.

In order to time the execution, I give the semaphore to the Task,  set
the  alarm  for the required duration and then go into a blocking wait
for the semaphore. If the semaphore  is  released  earlier,  the  wait
unblocks  and  I reset the  alarm and proceed. If the semaphore is not
released (the Task does not finish its job, I get the sigAlarm  signal
after the timer expires and I send the SigUsr1 signal to the task.

Now, for the problem.

If the Task finishes it's processing AND  within  a  very  short  time
(like 10ms) the timer expires THEN the sem_take function, which should
unblock (since cronologically the semaphore was given  first  and  the
timer  expired  later), actually 'breaks' indicating that the sem_take
was interrupted by a signal. This leads me to believe  that  the  Task
has  not finished it's job and thus not released  the semaphore. So, I
send it a signal and expect the semaphore back from it (in it's signal
hander).  But  this is a dead lock situation. The Task does not have a
semphore to give, while I keep waiting  for one.

The question here is, are there  any  latency  issues  involved  while
using  semphores and signals? If a semaphore is being transfered  from
one process to another AND a signal arrives at  this  time,   can  the
semaphore  operation  get  lost?  (This is what seems to  happen in my
case).

Would appreciate some help from someone out there.

You may either post your replys to this newsgroup or mail them to me.

Thanks in advance for any help, or even for any thought given to this
problem!

