X-Google-Thread: fee84,98971f9406c55d6c
X-Google-Attributes: gidfee84,public
From: Casper.Dik@Holland.Sun.COM (Casper H.S. Dik - Network Security Engineer)
Subject: Re: SCCS - Millenium compliance?
Date: 1997/04/16
Message-ID: <casper.861197123@uk-usenet.uk.sun.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 235197672
References: <stevew.292.000E0EA9@dougal.logica.co.uk>
Supersedes: <cancel.casper.861197123@uk-usenet.uk.sun.com>
Organization: Sun Microsystems, Netherlands
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


stevew@dougal.logica.co.uk (Steve Wyeth) writes:

>Given that this version of SCCS stores its dates in two-digit-year
>format, it seems reasonable to assume that it will have trouble
>once the year 2000 comes around.

It might; SCCS has a common lineage, they root has a year 2000 problem.

This problem is complicated by the fact that the SCCS fileformat is
portable and can be carried from vendor to vendor.

>If I had the source code to SCCS, I could probably sort it out
>(simple thingy to turn 11/10/89 into 11/10/1989 then modify
>'delta' etc to use four digit years).

The "quick fix" adopted by many Unix vendors until agreement is
reached on final format changes is to interpret dates < 70 as being
2000 + year and dates >= 70 as being 1900 + year. (or 69, not sure)

>Unfortunately the source code to SCCS doesn't appear to be
>public domain (there doesn't even seem to be a clone of it).

No, there isn't any; try and pry a patch out of your vendor (have you 
checked that it really doesn't work past 2000?)  Or upgrade, though that
would involved swapping hardware platforms, I suppose.

Casper
