potterveld@axmep1.phy.anl.gov () writes:

> 
> 
> I have two DEC alphas running OSF 3.2D that have been happily running on
> a ethernet network. They also have DEC fddi interfaces, between which I've
> strung fiber. I want to offload the very heavy NFS traffic between these
> machines to the fddi interfaces, but I can't them to use the fddi for
> anything. The physical link seems OK: "netstat -I fta0 -s" shows no errors.
> I think I need help with the configuration process.
> 
> I thought I could leave the old ethernet config alone, configure in the
> fddi interfaces with the same IP name/address, and define a static route
> between the two machines, so that packets from one machine to the other
> would go via fddi, and packets addressed otherwise would take the tu0
> interface.
> 
> Is this possible? Can someone give advice?
> 
> Can I use the same IP name/address for the tu0 and fddi interfaces, or
> do I need separate ones for each interface?
> 
> Do I need to setup one or both machines as a router? Currently, neither
> is a router, gateway, or nameserver.
> 
> We are using subnets, our mask is 255.255.255.128, and we are using a
> gateway with a default route of 146.139.228.126. The configuration I
> tried was:
> 
> HOSTNAME = aspen.phy.anl.gov
> NUM_NETCONFIG = 2
> NETDEV_0 = tu0
> IFCONFIG_0 = 146.139.228.39 netmask 255.255.255.128
> NETDEV_1 = fta0
> IFCONFIG_1 = 146.139.228.39 netmask 255.255.255.128
> RWHOD = no
> ROUTED = no
> ROUTED_FLAGS =
> GATED = no
> GATED_FLAGS =
> ROUTER = no
> MAX_NETDEVS = 24
> 
> 
> A fallback position would be to disconnect one of the machines from the
> ethernet, and config the other machine to route packets between ethernet
> and fddi. But I couldn't get this to work either. Any suggestions?
> 
> Thanks!
> 
> - David Potterveld (potterveld@anl.gov)

If you just want to use FDDI for network traffic between these two
machines you should do the following:

HOSTNAME = aspen.phy.anl.gov
NUM_NETCONFIG = 2
NETDEV_0 = tu0
IFCONFIG_0 = 146.139.228.39 netmask 255.255.255.128
NETDEV_1 = fta0
IFCONFIG_1 = 10.0.0.1 netmask 255.0.0.0
RWHOD = no
ROUTED = no
ROUTED_FLAGS =
GATED = no
GATED_FLAGS =
ROUTER = no
MAX_NETDEVS = 24

The 10.0.0.x series of IP numeber is one of the series that are
reserved for internal use. Give the other host 10.0.0.2. Routing
between these hosts will function automatically. To make it easier to
use you might define names in /etc/hosts for both 10.0.0.1 and
10.0.0.2 (like aspen-fddi).

As long as you use these adresses for NFS mounting and so forth it
will work - I have used it successfully for quite some time.

/Erk

