From: "D. Sean Davidson" <davidson@zk3.dec.com>
Subject: Re: Limiting disk buffers
Date: 1997/04/04
Message-ID: <33456D0C.3F54@zk3.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 231788304
References: <01bc3f6a$964ad0a0$533fa8c2@dwigglaptop.infonet>
Organization: Digital UNIX Release Engineering
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


Setting these values in your /etc/sysconfigtab when you system reboots
will cause the unified buffer cache (ubc) to not use all available
memory for data file caching.  These values will basically cause ufs
and advfs to only use 14% of memory for ubc entries and flush the dirty
buffers when more cache is needed.  The 4% is the amount of memory used
for internal buffers and structures.

Sean

vm:
 ubc-minpercent = 3
 ubc-maxpercent = 14
 ubc-borrowpercent = 10
 ubc-maxdirtywrites = 1

vfs:
 bufcache = 4

advfs:
 AdvfsCacheMaxPercent = 4



