Dave Hargreaves <evxdmh@unix.ccc.nottingham.ac.uk> writes:

> 
> Does anyone know how to get some sound out of an AlphaStation 600 5/266
> because my system doesn't seem to have any wave devices although
> everthing seems to be in the right place. Does the kernel have to be
> rebuilt for the sound card or what?
> 
> Thanks
> 
> Dave Hargreaves

Well, if multimedia services are installed with the correct hardware
support (on an AlphaStation 200 this manifests itself by the kernel
saying something like 'Apr  1 18:00:25 elgar vmunix: msb0 at isa0'
'msb' is for Microsoft Sounf Board) you should be able to get some
sound out of your box.

Don't expect any device files tough - there won't be any. All sound
I/O is handled by the mme server which in turn is communicated to by
using the appropriate program or by using mme system calls. For
playing audio files there is /usr/bin/mme/audioplay that you can use.

For an overview over MME you could try 'man 1mms mme'.

BTW - don't despair if there is no device file for audio playback.
The audioplay program can read from standard input.

/Erik

