In article <33454134.B80DDF0@compfac.tch.harvard.edu> Matthew Temple <manager@compfac.tch.harvard.edu> writes:
>Given the following program fragment:
> <code with a large automatic array declaration deleted>
>
>The above program fails with a "segmentation fault" and a core dump
>under Digital Unix 3.2C when compiled with either CC or GCC.   The SAME
>PROGRAM, when compiled on a DU 4.0B machine, runs successfully.    In
>fact, I can copy the executable from the 4.0B machine to a 3.2C machine
>and it will run successfully.    
>Can this be explained or fixed?

On 3.2 you are running against the stack-size limit. Do a

	unlimit stacksize

and it will run. The reason it works on 4.0 (and even with moving
the executable backwards) is that the stack expansion code and
other compiler support on 4.0 does the right thing even on the 3.2
system. Not a bug, rather a feature.

