|> On 29 Mar 97 04:28:00 GMT, "Andrew Liles" <aliles@IntNet.net> wrote:
|> >	I administer an IRC server which is running OSF/1 3.2. Unfortunately
|> >people like to abuse it, and recently we've been having problems with
|> >spoofed clients. I was wondering if there is any way to modify the TCP
|> >initial seqnum generator so that it chooses more random numbers. Currently
|> >it just increments each one by 64000, which is quite predictable and makes
|> >a host vulnerable to spoof attacks. Any help would be appreciated.
|> 

Peter Mayne replied:
|> Upgrade to V4.0.

I checked with Peter and the Digital UNIX TCP engineers; this is not
sufficient.  V4.0 does *not* randomize the TCP initial sequence
numbers.

However, a patch for this problem has already been developed and
is being tested.  I do not know when it will be released.

The patch probably will not be available for V3.2, and so you might
have to upgrade anyway.  There are many other reasons to upgrade
from V3.2 to the latest release (V4.0B); the engineers have done
a lot of work to improve TCP performance, as well as some security
fixes.

-Jeff



