It's not really an inode problem.  It's a problem with the size of the
metadata extents.  I had the same problem with my news spool.  It kept
reporting disk full, even when it wasn't.

I had to manually delete  some files from  the fileset so I could  get
expire to run. (remember  expire creates a list of files to delete
before it actually deletes anything.)

The solution was to remove the old file domain and recreate it with a
really big metadata size 

Here is the appropriate entry from the mkfdmn man page..


  Systems with file domains that contain very large numbers of files can use
  more metadata extents (similar to inodes in UFS) than normal.  By default,
  AdvFS attempts to grow the metadata table by 128 pages each time additional
  metadata extents are needed. Frequent requests by the system to increase
  the metadata table causes the metadata to become very fragmented which can
  result in an "out of disk space" error.

I don't use UFS, I can't wait for fsck after a power failure!


